speechtext:
Mr. Woodsworth moves in amendment to the main motion, seconded by Mr. Shaw:
That all the words after "that" be struck out and the following substituted therefor:-
Whereas the budget proposals would seem to indicate the recognition by the government of the necessity for a fiscal policy designed to encourage industries based upon the development of the natural resources of the country, and to afford some alleviation of the financial burdens bearing upon the people;
And whereas the condition of such industries and the position of consumers in general is such as to demand relief
Therefore, be it resolved:-
That the best interests of Canada will be served by an immediate and substantial reduction of the tariff on the necessaries of life including foodstuffs, clothing and building materials;
And further that it is desirable,
(a)	That the income tax should be so readjusted as to bear more heavily on unearned incomes;
(b)	That unimproved land values including all natural resources should not be exempt from taxation; and
(c)	That a graduated inheritance tax on large estates should be considered.

(See Resolution of the House, ji. 1434}
THE LAW MEMORIAL [Amongst those who perished in the destruction of the parliament buildings by fire on the night of Thursday, February 3, 1916, was Bowman Brown Law, representative of the county of Yarmouth, Nova Scotia. The tragic death of Mr. Law called forth expressions of sympathy and high esteem, and as time went on and the new parliament buildings neared
The Law Memorial
